staleTempT -- SEQ 432 (2026-10-27): F-144, an arithmetic operator that cannot use its operand hands back the SHARED
tempField -- the previous computation's value -- instead of its named refusal. Measured in kant (plain statements).

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds one input per row: everything above this file's `stop();`, then a define with
one action R = `sr = 9; <pre> sr = <expr>;`, then R(), a value line and a sentinel. One process per row, because the
control needs a process in which no computation has run before it: tempField is never cleared, so a control placed after
any stale row would read that row's leak.

Each row reads sr after the statement, and counts the REFUSED lines (H4: presence with a value, never absence).
gg is declared and holds no data, so no arm of + - * / can use it; sab is a STRING, which unary - cannot negate.
Stale rows: pre = `sx = 70 + 7;` leaves 77 in tempField. Today sr reads 77 and nothing refuses; intended sr keeps 9 and
the operator refuses by name. Controls: pre = `sx = 77;` computes nothing -- they refuse today and must refuse after.

